{
  "$comment": "What a search of resources by text answers, as every implementation of the Librarian must answer it: what the search is given, what each store answers, and the page. Semiont's Librarian (apps/librarian) runs every case and fails on a difference; a Librarian in another language runs the same file. A case has one of two parts, `search` or `preview`. WHAT A SEARCH STATES. `filters` are what the search is given: its `search` text and, where given, `archived`, `entityType`, `offset` and `limit`. `semanticFloor` is the floor the Librarian is configured with. Each store the search asks is stated with what it is `asked` and with what it `answers`, or that it `fails`. A store a case does not state is not asked, and a runner fails if it is. `graph` is the graph's listing of resources: it answers one page of `resources` and the `total` of all that match. `embedding` is the embedding of a text: it is asked with the text and answers a vector. `index` is the vector index's search of the chunks of resources: it is asked with an `embedding` and a `limit`, and with `entityTypes` where it is to answer chunks of resources of those types alone, and answers hits, one for a chunk, each with its `id`, the `resourceId` of the resource the chunk is of, its `score` and the chunk's `text`. `hydrated` is the hits read back as resources, one entry for each resource read, in the order they are read: `graph` is what the graph answers for that resource, a description or null, and `view` is what the views answer for it, a view or null. An entry with no `view` says the views are not asked for that resource. `page` is the answer: its `resources`, its `total` and its `matchKind`. `fails` in place of a page says the search fails. THE RULE OF A SEARCH. The text is searched without the spaces before and after it. A text that is then empty finds nothing: the page has no resources, a total of 0 and is `lexical`, and no store is asked. Otherwise the graph is asked with the text, with `archived` where it is given, with `entityTypes` holding the one entity type where one is given, with the `offset`, which is 0 where none is given, and with the `limit`, which is 50 where none is given. Where the graph fails the search fails. Where the graph's total is more than 0, or the offset is more than 0, the page is the graph's answer and is `lexical`, and nothing else is asked. So it is for a search given `archived` as true: the index holds no chunk of an archived resource, and is not asked for one. Otherwise nothing matched on the first page, and the index answers. The text is embedded once. The index is asked with that embedding and a limit of four times the page's limit, and, where the search is given an entity type, for chunks of resources of that type alone. A search given `archived` as false is asked of it as one given no `archived` is: every chunk it holds is of a resource that is not archived. Its hits are folded to one for each resource: the hit with the highest score, with that chunk's text, and of two with the same score the one the index answered first. A resource is kept when its score is the floor or more. The kept are ordered by score, highest first, and by nothing else. `total` is how many are kept. The first `limit` of them are read back, each in turn: from the graph, and from the views where the graph has no such resource or fails. A resource neither has is left out of the page. Each resource of the page carries the text of its chunk as `content`. The page is `semantic`, and it is `semantic` where nothing is kept. A view that fails is a resource that is not read back: it is left out of the page, and the others are read. Where the embedding or the index fails, the answer is the page with no resources, a total of 0, and `lexical`: a search that found nothing does not become a failure. WHAT A PREVIEW STATES. The Librarian gives each resource of a lexical page a preview of its text as `content`, and leaves the `content` of a semantic page as it is. No case here holds which page gets previews: only what a preview is. `resources` are the resources. `served` is what a read of a resource's bytes answers, by the resource's id: the `text` the bytes are, in UTF-8, and the `contentType` they are served with, or that the read `fails`. A resource that `served` does not name is not read, and a runner fails if it is. `previews` is the `content` each resource is given, in the order of `resources`. THE RULE OF A PREVIEW. A resource has a preview when its first representation states where its bytes are (`storageUri`) and has a media type whose bytes are its text. Which those are is read from specs/src/media-types/registry.json: a type whose `textSource` is `decode`, and a `text/` type the registry does not list. Its bytes are read once, by the resource's id, and decoded as the content type they are served with says. The preview is the first 200 characters of that text, and the whole of a text that is shorter. Any other resource previews as the empty string and its bytes are not read: one with no representation, one whose representation states no `storageUri`, a PDF, whose text is derived and not decoded, and an image, which has no text. A read that fails previews as the empty string, and the other resources keep theirs. No text of these cases has a character outside the Basic Multilingual Plane. Add a case here before changing an implementation.",
